[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: csw1
diagnosis_text:
Physical link on the CSW1<->R1 segment is healthy; the issue is a logical OSPF configuration on CSW1. Both ends report the interface up with carrier: ethtool shows “Link detected: yes” on csw1 eth0 and r1 eth0 at 10Gb/s, and direct IP reachability over the link is proven by successful pings in both directions (csw1 eth0 10.0.0.34 <-> r1 eth0 10.0.0.33). Despite this, the OSPF adjacency on that link is missing. On CSW1, the FRR running config has “interface eth0” with “ip ospf passive,” which suppresses OSPF hellos and prevents forming neighbors on eth0. Correspondingly, “show ip ospf neighbor” on csw1 shows no neighbor on eth0, and r1 shows a single OSPF neighbor only via eth1 (to CSW2), explaining why Internet traffic is using the CSW2 path exclusively and why monitoring flagged the CSW1<->R1 link from a routing perspective.

Localization: CSW1, interface eth0, OSPF process. Mechanism: passive OSPF on csw1 eth0 halts adjacency, so R1 loses the OSPF neighbor over WAN_LINK_1 and converges to CSW2. Root cause: a routine change inadvertently set CSW1’s eth0 to OSPF passive.

Corrective fix: On CSW1, remove the passive setting on eth0 (under interface eth0: “no ip ospf passive” or under router ospf: “no passive-interface eth0”), then verify “show ip ospf neighbor” forms adjacency with R1. No re-cabling is required. === END ===